  <h2 id="budget">How much of your MBA budget one course should eat</h2>

  <p>
    An MBA is already a significant financial commitment, especially at a top-tier institute where
    fees can run into several lakhs of rupees a year before living costs. As a strict planning
    heuristic, not a universal rule, cap what you spend on any single outside course or certification
    at roughly 10% of the total budget you have realistically set aside for skill-building and
    education across your MBA years. Treat spending materially above that, say past 20%, as a serious
    caution point that needs specific justification: a genuinely required credential for your target
    role, verified strong outcomes with real numbers attached, or access to supervised practice you
    cannot get elsewhere.
  </p>

  <p>
    "Everyone in my batch is doing the CFA" or "this provider is well known" is brand-prestige
    reasoning, not course-specific evidence. A cheaper, well-reviewed course that closes the same
    specific skill gap, chosen because you checked the syllabus and instructor yourself, usually
    beats an expensive one chosen because a coaching WhatsApp group recommended it.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Before paying for any course, check with your MBA institute's career services or placement
      cell. A meaningful number of B-schools already have institutional access to LinkedIn Learning,
      Coursera for Campus, or discounted CFA/PMP prep — often unused simply because students never
      ask.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sequencing">When to take these: before, during, or after the MBA</h2>

  <p>
    Timing matters more than most students plan for. A course taken at the wrong point in the MBA
    calendar either duplicates what you are about to learn in class or arrives too late to shape your
    summer internship or placement story.
  </p>

  <ol class="scorecard-list">
    <li class="scorecard-item">
      <span class="scorecard-item__circle">1</span>
      <div class="scorecard-item__body">
        <div class="scorecard-item__title">Before the MBA or in the first term: fill genuine background gaps</div>
        <p>
          If you are coming from a non-finance or non-quant background into a finance
          specialization, or a non-technical background into an analytics-heavy track, a short
          foundational course (Excel modeling basics, statistics fundamentals) before or in term one
          prevents you from drowning in core coursework later.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="scorecard-item">
      <span class="scorecard-item__circle">2</span>
      <div class="scorecard-item__body">
        <div class="scorecard-item__title">Between terms or over the summer: build the applied project</div>
        <p>
          This is the natural window for the specialization-specific certificate plus a real
          project — a campaign, a model, a dashboard — because you already have the core-course
          vocabulary and are not competing for time with active coursework.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="scorecard-item">
      <span class="scorecard-item__circle">3</span>
      <div class="scorecard-item__body">
        <div class="scorecard-item__title">In the final term or right after: target the specific interview gap</div>
        <p>
          Once you know the exact roles you are interviewing for, this is when narrow, high-signal
          prep — case practice for consulting, a final modeling sprint for finance, PMP once you are
          eligible — pays off fastest, because it is aimed at a specific, known interview process
          rather than a general skill.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="youtube">Free channels and resources worth using before you pay for anything</h2>

  <p>
    Try the free route on any narrow skill before committing money, especially for case-solving
    practice, where the best resources are genuinely free and updated constantly.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Case and consulting prep</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Hacking the Case Interview, Management Consulted, IGotAnOffer</div>
      <p>Between them: free step-by-step case walkthroughs and frameworks, a fuller view of consulting recruiting (resumes, networking, exit options), and mock interviews featuring former MBB interviewers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Structuring practice</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Crafting Cases, RocketBlocks</div>
      <p>Useful for building issue trees from first principles and drilling the math, structuring, and chart-reading sub-skills a live case tests, in short, focused videos rather than long lectures.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Official documentation and platform help centres</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Google Skillshop, Meta Blueprint, Excel/Power BI docs</div>
      <p>Free, current, and often more precise than a paid course covering the same tool, especially for platform-specific certifications in marketing and analytics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Combine two or three complementary free resources rather than relying on one, and treat two
    weeks of serious, honest effort on the free route as your real test: if it does not move you
    meaningfully closer to being useful in the target skill, that is a legitimate signal to pay for
    structure, not a failure on your part.
  </p>

