  <h2 id="proof">The certificate is not the proof — this is</h2>

  <p>
    Whatever course you choose, the certificate on its own rarely changes an interview outcome.
    What changes it is the specific, explainable thing you built while earning it: a three-statement
    model you can walk through line by line, a campaign with real numbers and a lesson learned from
    what did not work, a Six Sigma project with a measured before-and-after, or a people analytics
    dashboard built on a real or public dataset.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Treat every course as incomplete until it has produced one of these: a model, a campaign, a
      dashboard, a completed process-improvement project, or a case-solving track record you could
      demonstrate live. That combination — the right specialization skill, built and shown, on top
      of the MBA's breadth — is what actually compounds into higher-income opportunities and moves
      you toward earlier financial freedom faster than the MBA or the course could alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Do not try to evaluate every course in this article at once. Pick your specialization, check
    three to five real job postings for the specific role you are targeting after your MBA, and note
    which tool, platform, or credential shows up repeatedly. Then run that one specific course
    through the audit table above before paying anything.
  </p>

  <p>
    Picking the wrong specialization or role direction costs far more than picking the wrong course —
    it delays the entire skill portfolio this article is built around.
