  <h2 id="faq">FAQs on online courses for MBA students</h2>

  <div class="faq-list">
    <details class="faq-item" open>
      <summary class="faq-item__q">What are the best online courses for MBA students?</summary>
      <div class="faq-item__a">
        There is no single best course for every MBA student, because the right pick depends on your
        specialization and target role. Finance students usually get the most from a financial
        modeling and valuation course (such as Wharton's Business and Financial Modeling
        Specialization) or a CFA track; marketing students from Google's Digital Marketing &
        E-commerce Professional Certificate plus a real campaign; operations students from a Lean Six
        Sigma Green Belt or PMP depending on the role; and HR students from a people analytics
        certificate such as AIHR's or the SHRM/HRCI people analytics credential. Match the course to
        your specialization and a real project, not a generic ranked list.
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-item__q">Should MBA students do CFA alongside their MBA?</summary>
      <div class="faq-item__a">
        Only if you are targeting equity research, investment management, or FP&A roles specifically,
        and only after checking your realistic time budget. CFA Level 1 typically demands around 300
        hours of study with pass rates historically in the 35-45% range, and the full three-level
        charter usually takes candidates two-and-a-half to four years to complete. It complements an
        MBA rather than substituting for one: the MBA covers business breadth and networking, the CFA
        covers investment depth. Starting it casually alongside a demanding MBA term without a plan
        to finish all three levels often produces a half-finished credential.
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-item__q">Is PMP or Six Sigma better for an operations-focused MBA student?</summary>
      <div class="faq-item__a">
        They test different things. PMP proves you can plan, run, and close out a project end-to-end
        and requires either a bachelor's degree plus 36 months of project-leading experience or a
        high-school diploma plus 60 months, along with 35 hours of formal project management
        education, before you can even sit the exam. Six Sigma, especially a Green Belt, proves you
        can measure and improve an existing process and has a lower experience bar to start. If you
        are early in your MBA with limited work experience, a Six Sigma Green Belt with a real
        project is usually more realistic than attempting PMP before you are eligible.
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-item__q">Do online certificates actually help MBA graduates get hired in India?</summary>
      <div class="faq-item__a">
        Unevenly. A certificate issued or co-branded by a name recruiters already recognise in your
        target field — CFA Institute, PMI, Google, Meta, ASQ, SHRM — carries real weight, especially
        paired with a visible project. A stack of unrelated, unbranded certificates with nothing
        built alongside them tends to read as credential-collecting rather than genuine
        skill-building, and can work against a candidate in competitive interview processes. The
        project or outcome you can discuss usually matters more than the certificate name on its own.
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-item__q">How much should an MBA student spend on outside courses and certifications?</summary>
      <div class="faq-item__a">
        As a strict planning heuristic rather than a fixed rule, cap spending on any single outside
        course or certification at roughly 10% of your total realistic education and skill-building
        budget for the MBA years, and treat anything above 20% as needing specific justification such
        as a required credential for your target role or verified strong outcomes. An MBA already
        represents a large financial commitment; a certification should sharpen your direction inside
        that investment, not compete with the money you need for the degree itself, tools,
        internships, or a genuine job search.
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-item__q">Can AI tools replace the need for a specialization course?</summary>
      <div class="faq-item__a">
        No, and treating AI fluency as a substitute for domain depth is a common mistake. AI tools
        speed up parts of a workflow — drafting, first-pass analysis, research — but the judgment to
        direct that workflow, verify its output, and apply it to a real finance, marketing,
        operations, or HR problem still comes from the specialization skill itself. The strongest
        combination is a real specialization skill plus AI fluency applied to it, not AI fluency
        alone.
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

